  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s0",
      "title": "The question",
      "narration": "In **ΔABC**, **AB = 3 cm** and **∠B = 90°**. **Q** lies on **BC** such that **BQ = QC** (the tick marks) and **AQ = 5 cm**. We have to find (i) **BQ** and (ii) **AC**.",
      "actions": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "title": "Spot the right angle",
      "narration": "In ΔABQ, **∠B = 90°**, so the **hypotenuse** is the side opposite it: **AQ**. The other two sides meet at the right angle.",
      "actions": [
        {
          "pulse": [
            "ra0"
          ],
          "at": 700
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "title": "Pythagoras' theorem",
      "narration": "**Pythagoras' theorem**: the square on the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares on the other two sides.\\[\\begin{aligned}\\role{hyp}{AQ^2} &= \\role{legA}{AB^2} + \\role{legB}{BQ^2}\\end{aligned}\\]",
      "actions": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "title": "Make the unknown the subject",
      "narration": "We want a **shorter side**, so subtract the known square from the hypotenuse square.\\[\\begin{aligned}\\role{hyp}{AQ^2} &= \\role{legA}{AB^2} + \\role{legB}{BQ^2} \\\\ \\role{legB}{BQ^2} &= \\role{hyp}{AQ^2} - \\role{legA}{AB^2}\\end{aligned}\\]",
      "actions": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "title": "Substitute the lengths",
      "narration": "Replace each side by its length.\\[\\begin{aligned}\\role{hyp}{AQ^2} &= \\role{legA}{AB^2} + \\role{legB}{BQ^2} \\\\ \\role{legB}{BQ^2} &= \\role{hyp}{AQ^2} - \\role{legA}{AB^2} \\\\ &= \\role{hyp}{5^2} - \\role{legA}{3^2}\\end{aligned}\\]",
      "actions": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "title": "Square and subtract",
      "narration": "Square each number, then subtract.\\[\\begin{aligned}\\role{hyp}{AQ^2} &= \\role{legA}{AB^2} + \\role{legB}{BQ^2} \\\\ \\role{legB}{BQ^2} &= \\role{hyp}{AQ^2} - \\role{legA}{AB^2} \\\\ &= \\role{hyp}{5^2} - \\role{legA}{3^2} \\\\ &= \\role{hyp}{25} - \\role{legA}{9} \\\\ &= \\role{legB}{16}\\end{aligned}\\]",
      "actions": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "title": "Square root",
      "narration": "Take the square root of both sides (a length is positive).\\[\\begin{aligned}\\role{hyp}{AQ^2} &= \\role{legA}{AB^2} + \\role{legB}{BQ^2} \\\\ \\role{legB}{BQ^2} &= \\role{hyp}{AQ^2} - \\role{legA}{AB^2} \\\\ &= \\role{hyp}{5^2} - \\role{legA}{3^2} \\\\ &= \\role{hyp}{25} - \\role{legA}{9} \\\\ &= \\role{legB}{16} \\\\ \\role{legB}{BQ} &= \\sqrt{16} = \\role{ans}{4\\ \\text{cm}}\\end{aligned}\\]",
      "actions": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "title": "Find BC",
      "narration": "Q is the midpoint of BC (equal tick marks), so **QC = BQ = 4 cm**.\\[\\begin{aligned}BC &= BQ + QC \\\\ &= 4 + 4 = 8\\ \\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\\]",
      "actions": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "title": "Right angle and theorem",
      "narration": "In ΔABC, **∠B = 90°**, so the **hypotenuse** is the side opposite it: **AC**. The other two sides meet at the right angle. **Pythagoras' theorem**: the square on the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares on the other two sides.\\[\\begin{aligned}\\role{hyp}{AC^2} &= \\role{legA}{AB^2} + \\role{legB}{BC^2}\\end{aligned}\\]",
      "actions": [
        {
          "pulse": [
            "ra0"
          ],
          "at": 700
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "title": "Substitute and add",
      "narration": "Put in the lengths, square them and add.\\[\\begin{aligned}\\role{hyp}{AC^2} &= \\role{legA}{AB^2} + \\role{legB}{BC^2} \\\\ &= \\role{legA}{3^2} + \\role{legB}{8^2} \\\\ &= \\role{legA}{9} + \\role{legB}{64} \\\\ &= \\role{hyp}{73}\\end{aligned}\\]",
      "actions": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "title": "Square root",
      "narration": "73 is not a perfect square, so use a calculator and round to 3 significant figures.\\[\\begin{aligned}\\role{hyp}{AC^2} &= \\role{legA}{AB^2} + \\role{legB}{BC^2} \\\\ &= \\role{legA}{3^2} + \\role{legB}{8^2} \\\\ &= \\role{legA}{9} + \\role{legB}{64} \\\\ &= \\role{hyp}{73} \\\\ \\role{hyp}{AC} &= \\sqrt{73} = \\role{ans}{8.54\\ \\text{cm}}\\end{aligned}\\]",
      "actions": []
    }
  ],
  "formulas": [
    {
      "title": "Pythagoras' theorem",
      "tex": "\\role{hyp}{c^2} = \\role{legA}{a^2} + \\role{legB}{b^2}",
      "text": "In a right-angled triangle, the square on the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares on the other two sides."
    },
    {
      "title": "Finding the hypotenuse",
      "tex": "\\role{hyp}{c} = \\sqrt{\\role{legA}{a^2} + \\role{legB}{b^2}}",
      "text": "Add the two squares, then take the square root."
    },
    {
      "title": "Finding a shorter side",
      "tex": "\\role{legA}{a} = \\sqrt{\\role{hyp}{c^2} - \\role{legB}{b^2}}",
      "text": "Subtract the known shorter side's square from the hypotenuse's square, then take the square root."
    }
  ],
  "challenges": [
    {
      "id": "bc",
      "type": "number",
      "title": "Check BC",
      "answer": 8,
      "unit": "cm",
      "prompt": "If **BQ = QC = 4 cm**, how long is **BC**?",
      "hint": "BC is made of two equal parts.",
      "explain": "BC = BQ + QC = 4 + 4 = 8 cm."
    }
  ],
  "takeaways": [
    "Pythagoras' theorem works only in **right-angled** triangles — mark every right angle in the figure first.",
    "Split the figure into right-angled triangles and solve them **one at a time**; each answer feeds the next triangle.",
    "Keep a few extra decimal places in the middle of a calculation and round only the **final** answer (3 s.f.)."
  ],
  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "Exam tip",
      "text": "Always begin with a line such as \"In ΔABC, ∠C = 90°. Using Pythagoras' Theorem,\" — it shows which triangle and which right angle you are using."
    },
    {
      "title": "Rounding",
      "text": "If the answer is not a whole number, give it to **3 significant figures** unless the question says otherwise."
    }
  ]
}